 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the requirement to check the justice center's register of substantiated category one cases of abuse or neglect",
    "basePrintNo": "S7414",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7414-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§495 & 424-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7414\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to the\nrequirement to check the justice center's register of substantiated\ncategory one cases of abuse or neglect\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill would require additional service providers to check the\nStaff Exclusion List (\"SEL\") register of substantiated category one\ncases of abuse or neglect maintained by the Justice Center for the\nProtection of People with Special Needs (\"Justice Center\") before\nhiring employees or allowing persons to have regular and substantial\ncontact with service recipients, and would allow all providers to\nre-check it no more than once in any six-month period.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend subdivisions 2 and 3 of the Social\nServices Law (\"SSL\") § 495. A new paragraph (a) would be added to\nsubdivision 2 to add the following entities to the list of facilities\nand providers authorized to check the SEL: overnight, summer and\ntraveling summer day camps for children that are subject to article\nthirteen of the Public Health Law (PHL); nursing homes licensed by\nArticle 28 of the PHL, certified home health agencies, licensed home\ncare services agencies ,and long term home health care programs\ncertified under Article 36 of the PHL, and any adult home or enriched\nhousing program or residence for adults licensed under Article 7 of\nthe SSL.\n\nSection 1 would also add a subparagraph (b) to subdivision 2 to allow\nall providers required to check the SEL to re-check it no more than\nonce in any six-month period. It also would amend subdivision 3 of SSL\n§ 495 to require the additional entities defined in subdivision 2 to\nmake a decision about hiring an individual who is on the SEL in\naccordance with guidelines established by the Executive Director of\nthe Justice Center.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend SSL § 424-a(7) to clarify that a\nfacility or provider agency as defined by SSL § 488(4) that is\nrequired to check the SEL is not required to conduct a subsequent\ncheck of the Statewide Central Register for Child Abuse and\nMaltreatment maintained by the Office of Children and Family Services\nif the potential employee is already on the SEL.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would make it effective 60 days after it is\nsigned into law.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nUnder SSL § 495 the Justice Center is responsible for maintaining a\nregister of individuals who have been found to have a substantiated\ncategory one case of abuse or neglect, known as the Staff Exclusion\nList (\"SEL\"). Facilities and providers under the Justice Center's\njurisdiction as defined in SSL § 488(4), other providers of services\nto vulnerable persons in programs licensed, certified or funded by the\nOffice of Mental Health (OMH), the Office for People With\nDevelopmental Disabilities (OPWDD), the Office of Alcoholism and\nSubstance Abuse Services (OASAS), the Office of Children and Family\nServices (OCFS), the Department of Health (DOH) and the State\nEducation Department (SED) and other providers and licensing agencies\ndefined in SSL § 424-a(3) and (4) are all required to check the SEL\nprior to determining whether to hire an individual who will have the\n\npotential for regular and substantial contact with a service\nrecipient.\n\nSSL § 495 further provides that facilities or provider agencies that\nprovide services to vulnerable persons in programs licensed or\ncertified by OMH, OPWDD, OASAS, OCFS, DOH and SED shall not hire a\nperson who is on the SEL if that person would be in a position to have\nregular and substantial contact with a service recipient. For those\nproviders defined in SSL § 424-a(3) and (4), hiring decisions for\npersons on the SEL are governed by guidelines established by OCFS in\nconsultation with relevant state agencies, but there is no mandatory\nbar to hiring for these providers.\n\nA person can only be placed on the SEL after each allegation is\nthoroughly investigated and substantiated based upon a preponderance\nof evidence. The staff person has an opportunity to appeal the\ndecision before permanently being placed on the SEL. Requiring\nproviders to check the SEL prevents individuals who have already\ncommitted serious abuse or neglect against a vulnerable person from\nworking with them again.\n\nWhile many providers and facilities are required to check the SEL,\nthere are additional entities that provide services and care to\nvulnerable populations that should have access to this information\nabout potential employees. This proposed legislation would add nursing\nhomes, home care and long term home health programs, all children's\ncamps and all adult care facilities to the list of entities that must\ncheck the SEL. Currently, only children's camps that serve twenty\npercent or more of children with developmental disabilities and adult\nhomes or enriched housing programs that have 80 or more beds and in\nwhich at least twenty-five percent of the residents are persons with\nserious mental illness are required to check the SEL. Expanding access\nto the SEL to these settings will help to provide these facilities and\nproviders with critical information when making decisions about\nallowing an individual to interact with vulnerable persons. It also\nwould address the concern that persons on the SEL who are currently\nbarred from working in a facility or provider agency under the Justice\nCenter's jurisdiction will instead seek employment at a facility or\nprogram providing services to vulnerable individuals that is not\nrequired or authorized to check the SEL.\n\nThe legislation also includes language to authorize subsequent checks\nof the SEL by employers. Currently, there is no mechanism for a\nprovider or facility to check the SEL once an individual is hired.\nOften, individuals who provide services to vulnerable persons work in\nmore than one facility or program. If an individual is substantiated\nfor category one abuse or neglect in one setting, his or her other\nemployer may not have knowledge of that finding. Allowing regular\nchecks of the SEL by authorized entities would provide employers with\nimportant information about individuals working with vulnerable\npopulations. This language authorizing subsequent checks mirrors the\nlanguage that allows providers to re-check the Statewide Central\nRegister for Child Abuse and Maltreatment.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill would be implemented within the Justice Center's existing\nresources.\n\n LOCAL IMPACT :\n\nThis bill would have no impact on local governments.\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect 60 days after it is signed into law.\n",
